# GitHub Pages needs a public repo on the free tier, so everything committed here
# is world-readable. The print edition's exclusive content stays out of it.
#
# The twelve months of tasks are public no matter what — they have to be, they're
# inside the .ics files Google fetches. What the content draft adds on top is the
# part people are paying for: the Big Ticket Watch List lifespans, the "How to
# find out" page, the first-month checklist, and the design and pricing notes.
gulf-coast-maintenance-calendar-content.md

# Build artifacts: the PDF and its listing images, both rebuilt in a second by
# `python build_pdf.py`. Nothing here is held back — the PDF is a free download
# — but it is not served from docs/ either, so committing it would put a binary
# in the repo that nothing reads. Its source, product_content.py, is tracked,
# and so is the listing copy sitting beside these.
# The kit is the paid product and this repo is public, so neither the built PDF
# nor the HTML it is rendered from belongs in git. Both rebuild in a second from
# build_printables.py.
product/*.pdf
product/*.html
product/listing/

# Local Claude Code settings — machine-specific, not useful to anyone else.
# The .tmp.* pattern matters: Claude Code writes settings atomically via a temp
# file, and `git add -A` fails outright if it indexes one mid-write.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/*.tmp.*

__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The photographic master. optimize_images.py resizes it into the hero-*.jpg
# files that actually get served, so the 3 MB original would be dead weight in
# a repo that GitHub Pages serves. Keep it locally as the source of truth.
docs/img/hero.png

# Shop logo and banner: public marketing assets, but build artifacts all the
# same. build_brand.py remakes them in seconds, and binaries nothing in the
# repo reads do not belong in it.
product/brand/
